{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790704663,"nonce":"hWcvuYUotCL+NjiIzt4DfSZNcCA=","title":"Lower empty braces in explicit type checks as object values","message":"Issue7546 contains `eq(null, ({}:Dynamic).timeout)` and `var instance = ({}:Dynamic); eq(null, instance.field)`. The parser represents empty braces as an empty Block so the typer must use value context to turn them into an anonymous object. The TypeCheck lowering path called lower_expression on its inner expression without forwarding the declared type or value context. HIR therefore contained Block with no result, MIR omitted the field-read operand, and the generated assertion call had fewer arguments than its signature. Runtime comparison changes can make that particular assertion appear to pass, but they misclassify real object references.\n\nLower the inner expression through the existing value-expression path with the type check's target as expected type. This preserves an actual empty object for field access and keeps the generated call arity correct. Validate against Issue7546, authored regressions, and the full tiered report.","files":null}]}